<section id="early"><h2>From Perdriel to the Argentine countryside</h2><p>José Hernández was born on November 10, 1834, in Perdriel, in what is now the district of General San Martín near Buenos Aires. His parents were Rafael Hernández and Isabel Pueyrredón. The political disagreements of nineteenth-century Argentina were not remote abstractions in the world into which he was born: competing visions of how the country should be governed shaped families, institutions, and daily life.</p><p>Hernández studied in Buenos Aires, but formative years spent in the countryside brought him into contact with rural workers and the demands of cattle raising. Biographical research places him in the districts of Camarones and Laguna de los Padres, where the practical knowledge of horses and ranch work later visible in his writing took shape.</p><p>It would be misleading, however, to describe him simply as a gaucho who wrote down his experiences. Hernández was also a literate political actor and a practiced journalist. The voice of Martín Fierro was a literary creation, shaped by observation and by an argument about society.</p></section>
<section id="journalism"><h2>A journalist amid Argentina’s political struggles</h2><p>Hernández lived through disputes over the relationship between Buenos Aires and the Argentine provinces. Federalist ideas, struggles for political authority, and the consequences of warfare all marked his career. He took part in military and political life before becoming known as a newspaper writer whose arguments often defended the interests of people far from the centers of power.</p><p>His journalism took him through several cities, including Paraná and Corrientes. In 1869 he founded <em>El Río de la Plata</em>, a newspaper from which he denounced injustices affecting rural people. Reading Hernández as a public participant rather than an isolated poet helps explain why the complaints voiced in <em>Martín Fierro</em> have such political energy.</p><p>His positions were neither static nor reducible to a single modern label. He became a provincial legislator and senator and participated in debates about the organization of the country. The writer who criticized abuses of state authority also worked inside political institutions.</p></section>
<section id="poem"><h2>How Martín Fierro brought a gaucho’s voice to print</h2><p>In 1872 Hernández published <em>El gaucho Martín Fierro</em>, the first part of a narrative poem spoken by a rural horseman. Fierro describes losing his family life after forced military service on the frontier, suffering injustice, and coming into conflict with the authorities. The poem uses the rhythms and idioms associated with the gauchesque literary tradition, in which a crafted rural voice becomes the center of the story.</p><p>The 1879 continuation, <em>La vuelta de Martín Fierro</em>, brings new testimony from other characters and gives greater space to reunion and advice. The two parts should be read together but not flattened into one message: protest, exclusion, violence, responsibility, and accommodation are all present.</p><p>Hernández’s attention to gaucho speech did not turn the poem into a documentary transcript. As a work of art, it reflects choices about what could be said, what should be remembered, and whose suffering deserved a hearing. Its portrayal of Indigenous peoples and violence also requires historically informed, critical reading.</p><div class="related"><strong>Continue in English</strong><p><a href="/en/what-is-martin-fierro/">What Is Martín Fierro? Story, Meaning, and Historical Background</a></p><p><a href="/en/argentine-gauchos/">Who Were the Argentine Gauchos? History, Life, and Culture</a></p></div></section>
<section id="other"><h2>Other works, and a life beyond literature</h2><p>Martín Fierro is his most famous work, but it is not his entire bibliography. Hernández wrote about the federalist leader Ángel Vicente Peñaloza, known as the Chacho, in the 1860s. His later <em>Instrucción del estanciero</em> (1882) addressed the practical management of a rural estate—an important reminder that his interests were both literary and agricultural.</p><p>Correspondence provides another kind of evidence. A handwritten letter addressed to his wife, Carolina González del Solar, in April 1865 is available through the Biblioteca Pública de las Misiones. Unlike a published poem, such a document allows readers to see the author's personal handwriting and a moment from his family life. It remains part of the holding institution’s collection; our archive links to its reproduction rather than claiming ownership.</p><p>The distinction between a historical document, a later edition, and a modern transcription is central to understanding his legacy. A single author can leave many kinds of evidence, and each needs a clear provenance.</p></section>
<section id="legacy"><h2>Why José Hernández still matters</h2><p>Hernández died in Belgrano, Buenos Aires, on October 21, 1886. Later generations made his poem a central reference for thinking about Argentine identity, often celebrating the gaucho as a symbol of tradition and freedom. That recognition also created new debates: whether the figure was being romanticized, whether the social protest of the original had been softened, and whose rural histories were left outside the story.</p><p>Argentina observes the <em>Día de la Tradición</em> (Day of Tradition) on November 10, the anniversary of Hernández’s birth. Yet the richest way to honor his work is not simply to repeat its fame. It is to read the verses closely, compare them with historical evidence, and remain alert to the tensions that made the poem matter in the first place.</p></section>
